\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless otherwise noted, all experiments in this section use the v1 prompt (\S\ref{sec:model}; full templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}).

\subsection{Count-Degradation Curves}
\label{sec:count_curves}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_count_curves.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_bcs_scaling.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\caption{\textbf{(a)} Count-degradation curves across three tasks. Error bars show $\pm 1$\,SD across 5 random type batches per count level (omitted at count${}\,=\,1$ where each type is evaluated individually). NER recall and RE accuracy decline monotonically with schema size; MAVEN relaxed F1 increases (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). \textbf{(b)} BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. The BCS--accuracy slope is consistent across the independent 16-relation pool and within-pool 16-relation subsets (permutation $p = 0.60$); the wider BCS range available at 80 relations produces larger absolute gaps.}
\label{fig:main_results}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a shows extraction quality degrades monotonically with schema size across three datasets: Few-NERD recall drops from 0.72 to 0.42 ($-$42\%), FewRel accuracy from 85.7\% to 50.4\% ($-$35.3\,pp), and DocRED from 88.0\% to 38.7\% ($-$56\%). MAVEN exhibits a reversed trend attributable to centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). The effect replicates on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct ($-$43\,pp on Few-NERD; per-batch $r = 0.725$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}), indicating schema configuration rather than model-specific factors predicts difficulty. Count curves (5 batches per level) establish trend direction; magnitude estimation relies on the higher-powered BCS-controlled experiments (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\subsection{Pairwise Confusability and Type Confusion}
\label{sec:pairwise}

We test whether pairwise SCS predicts type-level confusion rates. For each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the confusion rate---the fraction of $t_i$ gold instances misclassified as $t_j$---and correlate it with $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)$. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports Pearson correlations with bootstrap 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Embed.} & \textbf{$n$ pairs} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$r^2$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & Qwen3 & 1{,}953 & 0.112 & 0.013 & {[}0.076, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
MAVEN & MiniLM & 11{,}026 & 0.129 & 0.017 & {[}0.104, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Pairwise SCS--confusion correlation. $r^2$ indicates the fraction of variance in type confusion explained by embedding similarity. Both correlations are modest in magnitude but robust across datasets and embedding models (both $p \ll 0.001$). Confusion rate for each ordered pair $(t_i, t_j)$ is computed as the total bidirectional confusion count $C(t_i{\to}t_j) + C(t_j{\to}t_i)$ divided by the sum of gold frequencies, aggregated over all count conditions; CIs are pair-level percentile bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$). We omit FewRel ($r = 0.187$; \S\ref{sec:scs-validation}) as RE's per-instance format precludes direct cross-task comparison of confusion rates. Type-level cluster bootstrap CIs: Few-NERD [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN [0.081, 0.185]---both exclude zero, confirming robustness to within-type correlation.}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

The correlations are statistically significant but modest: $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}1.7\%$. Type-level cluster bootstrap yields wider but still significant CIs (Few-NERD: [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN: [0.081, 0.185]; $B = 10{,}000$). Both datasets show significant positive correlations between pairwise SCS and type confusion rates. Spearman correlations corroborate: $\rho = 0.144\text{--}0.227$ across all pairs, strengthening to $\rho = 0.27\text{--}0.31$ among pairs with nonzero confusion (though conditioning on the dependent variable may inflate this).

The remaining variance likely reflects entity frequency, prompt position \cite{lost-in-middle-2024}, mention ambiguity, and definition quality. Although pairwise $r^2$ is modest, the signal compounds at the batch level into quartile gaps of 3.9--5.3\,pp under typical batching (\S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude}). Notably, the independent MiniLM embedding yields a \emph{stronger} SCS--confusion correlation on Few-NERD ($r = 0.172$ vs.\ Qwen3 $r = 0.138$; Appendix), suggesting that shared-representation bias is conservative rather than inflationary.

\subsection{BCS-Controlled Experiments}
\label{sec:bcs_controlled}

We fix type count and vary BCS. FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per type) eliminates type frequency as a confound; the within-pool analysis (\S\ref{sec:within_pool}) controls for pool composition.

In BCS-controlled FewRel subsets, the high-BCS quartile shows $\Delta=-5.3$\,pp lower accuracy than low-BCS (quartile comparison; full data in Appendix~\ref{app:bcs-controlled-table}). At 80 relations, extreme-batch construction amplifies this to $-$31.2\,pp (CI: [17.7, 42.7]), stable across count levels. An intermediate count${}\,=\,40$ evaluation (40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool) yields consistent gaps ($-$33.3\,pp at count 8, $-$31.8\,pp at count 16), confirming monotonic decline.

\paragraph{Document-level RE (DocRED).}
The effect replicates on DocRED's 96-relation schema: BCS gap $-$23.3\,pp at count 16 (slope $= -0.713$, $\rho = -0.661$, $p < 0.001$) and $-$17.6\,pp at count 32 (slope $= -0.690$, $\rho = -0.509$, $p = 0.002$). BCS explains 44--53\% of cross-batch variance ($R^2 = 0.525$ at count 16), substantially higher than FewRel ($R^2 = 0.218$), possibly reflecting a wider confusability range in the DocRED schema, though document-level context may also provide richer cues for type confusion. Across all RE settings, BCS slopes range from $-$0.40 to $-$0.71, significant in all conditions with sufficient BCS range.

\paragraph{Within-pool controlled comparison.}
\label{sec:within_pool}
Within-pool analysis confirms slope consistency across pools (permutation $p \approx 0.60$; Appendix~\ref{app:within-pool-method}). The 80-relation extreme-batch gap (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) reflects wider BCS range, not stronger per-unit sensitivity.

\paragraph{Practical magnitude.}
\label{sec:practical_magnitude}
For representative batches from the 80-relation pool ($n{=}100$ random 16-type subsets), the typical BCS span (IQR = 0.054) yields an expected accuracy gap of 2.2\,pp (95\% CI: [0.4, 4.0])---roughly 7\% of the extreme-batch gap. Quartile comparison confirms: bottom-quartile BCS batches average 82.8\% vs.\ 78.9\% for top-quartile ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp). The 31.2\,pp figure reflects maximum BCS contrast, not the typical batch experience. Conditional pair-level $R^2 \approx 7\%$ among non-zero confusion pairs (vs.\ unconditional $r^2 = 1.3$--$1.7\%$ across all pairs; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}; we note the conditioning inflates this estimate) aggregates to batch-level $R^2 \approx 22\%$ ($\sim$3$\times$ amplification, reproduced by Monte Carlo simulation accounting for inter-pair correlation structure; see Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim} for conditional vs.\ unconditional simulation comparison).

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
The effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: BCS gaps of $-$27.1\,pp (count 8) and $-$23.9\,pp (count 16), reaching 77--87\% of Qwen3-32B's gap. Both models converge to $\approx$51\% under high confusability regardless of count, while divergence is confined to the low-BCS regime---suggesting a task-inherent difficulty floor under high confusability (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). Channel~1 replicates fully on Llama-3.1-8B (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}); Channel~2 replicates on RE but shows floor effects on 8B NER (Limitations, item~1).

\paragraph{Prompt sensitivity.}
Under the v2 prompt, the extreme-batch BCS gap is $-$25.5\,pp (low-BCS 85.2\% vs.\ high-BCS 59.7\%; $n{=}25$; $r = -0.919$; $p < 0.001$)---modestly attenuated from v1's extreme-batch gap but highly significant. NER v2 yields consistent batch-level correlations ($r = +0.58$ vs.\ $r = +0.50$ under v1). Prompt design modulates magnitude but does not eliminate the effect (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_error_taxonomy.pdf}
\caption{Error taxonomy decomposition. IntraSub scales monotonically with BCS in both tasks. RE missed rates remain flat (10--16\%), but NER missed rates are substantially higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic.}
\label{fig:error_taxonomy}
\end{figure}

Error taxonomy decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} and Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy}) shows intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS: $\rho = 1.0$ ($p < 0.001$) for NER and $\rho = 0.954$ ($p < 0.001$) for RE. Under closed-set classification, non-\texttt{none} errors are necessarily intra-batch; the more informative finding is that IntraSub confusion frequency correlates with pairwise SCS ($\rho{=}0.954$, $p{<}0.001$). In NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic, indicating confusable types impair entity detection itself. Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches.

These results establish two channels: \textbf{Channel~1} (count predicts recall decline) and \textbf{Channel~2} (confusability predicts type confusion), cleanly separable in RE but coupled in NER where type ambiguity and boundary ambiguity interact. The full-range regression ($n = 69$, five count levels) serves as the primary separability test; the $2 \times 2$ factorial ($n = 54$) as a sensitivity check. TOST equivalence testing confirms additive separability (interaction = 0.47\,pp, $p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.003$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}).

\subsection{Centrality Confound on Homogeneous Schemas}
\label{sec:centrality_confound}

Per-type analysis on MAVEN reveals an anomalous \emph{positive} BCS--F1 correlation (Qwen3 $\rho = +0.184$, $p = 0.025$; MiniLM $\rho = +0.208$, $p = 0.011$). This traces to same-domain embedding collapse \cite{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}: MAVEN's FrameNet-derived types cluster tightly, so mean BCS conflates confusability with centrality---high-BCS types are semantically central and easy to extract. Controlling for log frequency and degree centrality, the positive correlation vanishes ($p > 0.2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}). The pairwise SCS signal remains intact ($r = 0.129$, $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}); the problem is per-type aggregation, not the underlying relationship. Few-NERD's cross-domain schema does not exhibit this confound ($\rho = -0.293$, $p = 0.02$). Mean BCS is thus reliable only for cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas require pairwise SCS or centrality adjustment.

\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

For each question, the answer is \textbf{Yes}, \textbf{No}, or \textbf{N/A}. If Yes, the relevant section is indicated. If No, a justification is provided.

\subsection*{A \quad For Every Submission}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{A\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you describe the limitations of your work?}

Yes. The Limitations section (after \S\ref{sec:conclusion}) discusses single-model reliance, modest pairwise effect sizes, cross-pool slope consistency, factorial interaction tests with TOST equivalence, decomposition scope, and metric scope.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?}

N/A. This is a purely analytical study of schema-induced degradation in existing LLM IE systems. It does not introduce new models, datasets, or deployment-facing artifacts. The findings characterize failure modes and are intended to inform safer schema design.

\item \textbf{Do the abstract and introduction summarize the paper's main claims?}

Yes. The abstract and \S\ref{sec:introduction} state the two-channel degradation pattern (count and confusability), the cross-model consistency check, and the negative SDGD result.

\item \textbf{Did you use AI assistants (e.g., ChatGPT, Copilot) when working on this paper?}

Yes. An AI coding assistant was used for experiment scripting and LaTeX formatting. All scientific claims, experimental design, and analysis were conducted by the authors.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{B \quad Scientific Artifacts}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{B\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you cite the creators of artifacts you used?}

Yes. Few-NERD \cite{fewnerd-2021}, FewRel \cite{fewrel-2018}, DocRED \cite{docred-2019}, MAVEN \cite{maven-2020}, Qwen3-32B \cite{qwen3-2025}, Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \cite{llama3-2024}, vLLM \cite{vllm-2023}, and all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \cite{minilm-2020} are cited in \S\ref{sec:datasets} and \S\ref{sec:model}.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the license or terms for use and/or distribution of any artifacts?}

Yes. All four datasets are publicly available research benchmarks. Few-NERD is released under CC-BY-SA 4.0; FewRel, DocRED, and MAVEN are publicly released for research use. Models are used under their respective open-weight licenses.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss if your use of existing artifacts is consistent with their intended use?}

Yes. All datasets are used for their intended purpose (IE evaluation). No data is used outside the scope specified by the dataset creators.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss whether the data contains personally identifiable information or offensive content?}

N/A. We use established benchmarks that have been vetted by the research community. No new data collection or annotation was performed.

\item \textbf{Did you provide documentation of the artifacts, e.g., coverage of languages, coverage of domains, etc.?}

Yes. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} reports type counts, evaluation unit counts, domain characteristics, and BCS ranges for each dataset. \S\ref{sec:datasets} describes dataset provenance and sampling procedures.

\item \textbf{Did you report relevant statistics for your dataset(s)?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:datasets} and Table~\ref{tab:datasets} report evaluation set sizes, type counts, and sampling methodology. Count levels and batch sizes are specified in \S\ref{sec:exp_design}.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{C \quad Computational Experiments}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{C\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you report the number of parameters in the models used, the total computational budget, and computing infrastructure?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:model}: Qwen3-32B (32B parameters, FP8) and Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (8B parameters). Inference was conducted on a single RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell GPU (96\,GB) served via vLLM. Total compute was approximately 48 GPU-hours of inference (no training).

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the experimental setup, including hyperparameter search and best-found values?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:exp_design} describes both experimental paradigms (count curves and BCS-controlled). \S\ref{sec:model} reports decoding parameters (greedy, temperature${}=0$, seed${}=42$) and prompt versions. No hyperparameter search was conducted as this is a zero-shot evaluation study.

\item \textbf{Did you report descriptive statistics about your results?}

Yes. All key metrics include 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals (10{,}000 resamples, percentile method) as stated in \S\ref{sec:model}. Spearman correlations are reported with $p$-values throughout \S\ref{sec:count_curves}--\ref{sec:centrality_confound}. Permutation tests are used for slope comparisons.

\item \textbf{Did you report implementation details, e.g., versions of packages?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:model} specifies vLLM v0.20.2 for model serving. Embedding models are named with version identifiers (\S\ref{sec:scs}). Code and scripts will be released upon acceptance.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{D \quad Human Annotators or Crowdworkers}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{D\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you report the full text of instructions given to participants?}

N/A. No human annotation or crowdsourcing was conducted.

